.TH DIFF 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
diff \- compare two files or directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diff
.RB [ \-c | \-e | \-f | \-u ]
.RB [ "\-C n | \-U n" ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diff
compares
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
and reports the differences needed to change
.I file1
into
.IR file2 ,
in one of five formats: the default (an ed-script-like listing), or,
if one of
.BR \-c ", " \-C ", " \-e ", " \-f ", " \-u ", or " \-U
is given, one of the others described under Output formats below.
.PP
If both operands are ordinary files (or one is standard input, given
as
.BR \- ),
that comparison happens directly. If both are directories,
.B diff
compares their contents: a name present in only one directory is
reported as such; a name that is a regular file in both is compared as
above; a name that is a directory in both is either recursed into
(with
.BR \-r )
or reported as a common subdirectory without being compared further
(without
.BR \-r );
a name that is a directory in one and not the other is reported as
such; a name that is anything other than a regular file or a directory
in both (a block or character device, or a FIFO) is skipped without
comment. If exactly one operand is a directory,
.B diff
compares the other operand against the file inside that directory
whose name is the other operand's own last path component.
.SS Output formats
.TP
Default
A sequence of hunks, each introduced by a header line naming the
affected line(s) of
.I file1 ,
a single letter
.RB ( a
for lines appended,
.B d
for lines deleted,
.B c
for lines changed), and the affected line(s) of
.I file2
.RI ( Na ", " Nd ", or " Nc ,
or
.IR M , Na ", " M , Nd ", or " M , Nc
for a range of more than one line).
.IR file1 's
own lines in the hunk follow, each prefixed with a
.B <
and a space; then, if the hunk has lines on both sides, a separator
line of three hyphens; then
.IR file2 's
lines, each prefixed with a
.B >
and a space.
.TP
.B "\-e (ed script)"
A script of
.BR ed (1)
.BR a / c / d
commands that would turn
.I file1
into
.I file2
if given to
.BR ed (1),
ordered so that a later command never invalidates a line number an
earlier one still refers to.
.TP
.B \-f
Like
.BR \-e ,
but hunks are given in the opposite order, the command letter comes
before its line range rather than after, and a multi-line range is
written as two numbers separated by a space rather than a comma.
.TP
.BR \-c " or " \-C " n (context format)"
A header naming both files and their modification times (the current
time, for standard input), followed by one block per group of nearby
hunks: a line of the form
.IR "***\ n1,n2\ ****"
giving
.IR file1 's
line range, then
.IR file1 's
own lines (each prefixed with two spaces if unchanged context, a
hyphen and a space if only
.I file1
has them, or an exclamation point and a space if changed on both
sides), a line of the form
.IR "\-\-\-\ n1,n2\ \-\-\-\-"
giving
.IR file2 's
line range, and
.IR file2 's
own lines with the same prefix convention, using a plus sign and a
space for lines only
.I file2
has.
.I n
(default 3, or given directly by
.B \-C
.IR n ,
which must be a positive integer) is the number of unchanged lines of
context kept around each hunk; hunks whose context would otherwise
overlap or touch are merged into one block sharing it.
.TP
.BR \-u " or " \-U " n (unified format)"
Like context format, but each block is a single header line of the
form
.IR "@@\ \-n1,len1\ +n2,len2\ @@"
(the second and fourth numbers are line counts, not the last line's
own number) followed by one line per line in the block, each prefixed
with a <blank> (context), a hyphen (only in
.IR file1 ),
or a plus sign (only in
.IR file2 ),
with no separate before/after sub-blocks.
.I n
(default 3, or given directly by
.B \-U
.IR n ,
which must be zero or a positive integer) behaves the same as
.BR \-C 's.
.PP
For context and unified format, a name-and-timestamp line introduces
each file: for context format, a line of the form
.IR "***\ file1\ <date>"
and a line of the form
.IR "\-\-\-\ file2\ <date>" ;
for unified format, a line of the form
.IR "\-\-\-\ file1\ <date>"
and a line of the form
.IR "+++\ file2\ <date>" .
.PP
A line that is the last line of its file and has no trailing newline
is followed, in the default, context, and unified formats, by a line
reading "\e No newline at end of file". Neither the
.B \-e
nor the
.B \-f
script can represent that fact at all; when it would otherwise apply,
this implementation instead reports it as a diagnostic to standard
error and raises the exit status to 2 (see EXIT STATUS).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Ignore trailing whitespace on each line, and treat any nonempty run of
spaces or tabs at the same position in two lines as equal to any other
nonempty run there, regardless of how many characters make it up on
either side. A context or unified line shown as unchanged under this
rule always displays
.IR file1 's
own exact text.
.TP
.B \-c
Use context format, with the default of 3 lines of context.
.TP
.BI \-C " n"
Use context format with
.I n
lines of context;
.I n
must be a positive integer.
.TP
.B \-e
Use ed script format.
.TP
.B \-f
Use the alternative ed-like format described above.
.TP
.B \-r
When comparing two directories, recurse into subdirectories present
under the same name in both, instead of merely reporting them as
common subdirectories.
.TP
.B \-u
Use unified format, with the default of 3 lines of context.
.TP
.BI \-U " n"
Use unified format with
.I n
lines of context;
.I n
must be zero or a positive integer.
.PP
.BR \-c ", " \-e ", " \-f ", " \-u ", " \-C ", and " \-U
are mutually exclusive; giving more than one is refused.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.IR file1 ", " file2
The two files (or directories, or one of each, per DESCRIPTION above)
to compare. Either operand, but not both, may be
.B \-
for standard input, which is read as if it were a regular file with
the current time as its modification time.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.BR \-i ", " \-l ", " \-n ", " \-s ", and " \-w ,
options some other
.B diff
implementations accept, are not implemented here; any unrecognized
option is refused with a diagnostic and exit status 2, not silently
ignored.
.PP
.B diff
computes its edit script using an algorithm whose time and memory both
scale, in the worst case, with the square of the combined size of the
two inputs. This is fine for ordinary text files, but not intended for
comparing extremely large or almost entirely dissimilar inputs.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No differences were found.
.TP
.B 1
Differences were found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (invalid usage, a file or directory that could not
be opened, read, or stat'd, or an out-of-memory condition), or, with
.B \-e
or
.BR \-f ,
the produced script could not faithfully represent a missing trailing
newline in one of the inputs (see Output formats above).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmp (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR ed (1)
